    <!-- Step 1: consent. -->
    <string name="consent_title">Before you connect</string>
    <string name="consent_intro">Kumiho runs a VPN on this phone. While it is connected, the traffic of the apps that use the tunnel goes to a Kumiho proxy server, and that server reaches the sites you asked for. This is what keeps your address away from those sites and what makes the tunnel work.</string>
    <string name="consent_future">Android asks for VPN permission in a later step. Nothing is routed until you grant it and connect.</string>
    <string name="consent_local_title">What stays on this phone</string>
    <string name="consent_local_tokens">Your Firefox Account tokens are written only to Kumiho\'s private app storage. Android backup and device transfer are turned off for this app, so they are never copied off the phone.</string>
    <string name="consent_local_data">Settings, the server list and the connection logs are local files in the same private storage. A log leaves the phone only when you share it yourself.</string>
    <string name="consent_local_no_telemetry">There is no telemetry, no analytics and no crash reporting. Nothing is sent to the Kumiho developers.</string>
    <string name="consent_proxy_title">Local proxies</string>
    <string name="consent_proxy_text">While the tunnel is up, Kumiho also listens on this phone\'s loopback interface for its local SOCKS5 proxy, and for its local HTTP proxy when you turn that on. Any app on this phone can use them, so treat them as device-wide. Other devices on your network cannot reach them.</string>
    <string name="consent_accept">I understand what Kumiho routes, and that I am responsible for what I send through it.</string>

    <!-- Step 2: sign in. -->
    <string name="sign_in_intro">Kumiho uses your Firefox Account entitlement to reach the proxy network. The engine talks to Firefox Accounts directly, with no browser hand-off.</string>
    <string name="sign_in_email_label">Firefox Account email</string>
